# SRG-APP-000100-WSR-000064  -  CAT II
# The web server must produce log records containing sufficient information
# to establish the identity of any user/subject or process associated with
# an event.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations, then kept
# by hand. Edit this file to change how this requirement is assessed here;
# it is read at run time. Regenerating refreshes the benchmark text in place
# and leaves the criteria, the determination and any verification block as
# they are.

id:              SRG-APP-000100-WSR-000064
group:           V-206365
severity:        medium
cat:             CAT II
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       machine
confidence:      derived
cci:             CCI-001487
control_rev5:    AU-3

contribution:    Splunk's web access logs name the authenticated user for each request, and
                 the audit trail names the account behind each operation. Where an
                 authenticating proxy supplies the identity, web.conf remoteUser names the
                 header that carries it, remoteUserMatchExact = 1 stops the
                 dash-and-underscore header collision the specification warns of - under
                 which "the browser could dictate the username" - and trustedIP restricts
                 which proxy may assert it.

governs:         audit.conf - The audit trail Splunk keeps of its own activity, and how those records
                 are signed.
governs:         restmap.conf - The management interface endpoints, and which clients may reach them.
governs:         web.conf - Configures the Splunk Web interface.

check:           restmap.conf [<rest endpoint name>:<endpoint description string>] includeInAccessLog not_equals false
check:           audit.conf [auditTrail] queueing not_equals false
# --- vendor verification: generated, do not edit by hand
vendor:          web.conf [settings] log.access_file https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/web.conf
retired_check:   web.conf [settings] log.access_file non_empty
                 reason: "log.access_file = <filename>. The HTTP access log filename. This
                         file is written in the default $SPLUNK_HOME/var/log directory.
                         Default: web_access.log" Related shipped settings:
                         log.access_maxsize = 25000000 and log.access_maxfiles = 5.
# --- end vendor verification

external:        Where single sign-on is in use, web.conf remoteUserMatchExact must be set
                 to 1 and trustedIP must name the authenticating proxy, as the
                 specification directs for an SSO setup. Those two settings bear on this
                 requirement only in an SSO deployment, so they are reported here rather
                 than tested unconditionally - a non-SSO instance legitimately leaves both
                 at their defaults, and failing it for them would be a false finding.
external:        The authenticating proxy or identity provider establishes the identity it
                 asserts; the application records the identity it is given.
external:        The user field is emitted by the product in a fixed record format with no
                 configuration surface.

citation:        SRG-APP-000100-WSR-000064 check text; web.conf.spec:728-748 (remoteUser =
                 <http_header_string>, Default: REMOTE_USER, with the header-normalization
                 caution), :767-776 (remoteUserMatchExact = [0 | 1], "Set to 1 when you set
                 up SSO", Default: 0), :797-804 (trustedIP = <ip_addresses>, "Set to a
                 valid IP address to enable SSO", Default: not set), :871-874
                 (log.access_file); restmap.conf.spec:138-142 (includeInAccessLog =
                 <boolean>, Default: true); audit.conf.spec:23-29 ([auditTrail], queueing =
                 <boolean>, Default: true)
finding:         Splunk Enterprise has no web access log configured, excludes REST
                 endpoints from splunkd_access.log, or has audit queueing disabled - and,
                 where single sign-on is configured, leaves remoteUserMatchExact at 0 or
                 trustedIP unset - so its web records do not reliably establish the
                 identity of the user or process associated with the event.
